```c
static int decode_bit_field(const u8 * inbuf, size_t inlen, void *outbuf, size_t outlen, const int strict)
{
        u8              data[sizeof(unsigned int)];
        unsigned int    field = 0;
        int             i, n;

        if (outlen != sizeof(data))
                return SC_ERROR_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL;

        n = decode_bit_string(inbuf, inlen, data, sizeof(data), 1, strict);
        if (n < 0)
                return n;

        for (i = 0; i < n; i += 8) {
                field |= ((unsigned int) data[i/8] << i);
        }
        memcpy(outbuf, &field, outlen);
        return 0;
}
```

`data` is `u8 data[sizeof(unsigned int)]` = 8 bytes on 64-bit. `decode_bit_string` checks `if (outlen < octets_left) return SC_ERROR_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL;` where `outlen = sizeof(data) = 8`. So `octets_left` can be at most 8. `n = (count * 8) - zero_bits` where `count = octets_left`. So `n` can be at most `8*8 - 0 = 64`. The loop `for (i = 0; i < n; i += 8)` reads `data[i/8]` for `i = 0, 8, ..., 56`, so `data[0]` through `data[7]` - that's within bounds for 8-byte data.

